  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "Find the set of values of $x$ that satisfy $-1<2x-3\\le 1$ and $3x>9.$",
  "worked_solution": "To solve compound \"and\" inequalities, we solve each inequality separately and then find their intersection.\n\n- First, we solve $-1<2x-3\\le 1\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & <2x-3\\le 1 \\\\ -1+3 & <2x-3+3\\le 1+3 \\\\ 2 & <2x\\le 4 \\\\ \\frac{2}{2} & <\\frac{2x}{2}\\le \\frac{4}{2} \\\\ 1 & <x\\le 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14194110/assets/7f940af7e7480f83d89f47b53ec1f9b9d9d23e93d8419464055ada7b77c22be1.png)\n\n- Then, we solve  $3x>9\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & >9 \\\\ \\frac{3x}{3} & >\\frac{9}{3} \\\\ x & >3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14194110/assets/0883c587c9c164cc35fdf17b07f0d0bd3767c715c8c537e3c1013239194f22bc.png)\n\nTo find the values of $x$ that satisfy both inequalities, we find where the two solutions overlap:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14194110/assets/5d31a9ebff15e2eb52faf5e3768576cf2de60205882fc130efb57f77ffad0b96.png)\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nIt turns out that the solutions do not overlap at all. Therefore, there are no values of $x$ that satisfy both inequalities simultaneously. So, there is no solution.",
